 growth characteristics along the horizontal axis. Different investment styles often have different levels of risk and lead to differences in returns. Therefore, it is crucial that investors understand style and have a tool to measure their style exposure.",RR01Y:i.Screener.annotation_rr01y||"The Morningstar Rating for funds, commonly called the star rating, is a measure of a fund's risk-adjusted return, relative to similar funds. Funds are rated from one to five stars, with the best performers receiving five star and the worst performers receiving a single star. ",ST167:i.Screener.annotation_st167||"The average daily trading volume of common shares over the specified time period. This is expressed in number of shares. The daily volume is collected, but the average daily volume is calculated.",OS02W:i.Screener.annotation_os02w||"The average daily trading volume of common shares over the specified time period. This is expressed in number of shares. The daily volume is collected, but the average daily volume is calculated.",AA0F9:i.Screener.annotation_aa0f9||"This is the number of shares or contracts traded for a security in one day. ",ST755:i.Screener.annotation_st755||"The growth in the equity's net income on a percentage basis",STA99:i.Screener.annotation_sta99||"The growth in the equity's revenue on a percentage basis",ST263:i.Screener.annotation_st263||"The growth in the equity's earnings per share from continuing operations on a percentage basis.",PM006:i.Screener.annotation_pm006||"Expressed in percentage terms, Morningstar's calculation of total return is determined each month by taking the change in monthly net asset value, reinvesting all income and capital-gains distributions during that month, and dividing by the starting NAV. Reinvestments are made using the actual reinvestment NAV, and daily payoffs are reinvested monthly. We provide both current and month-end returns",AA0FP:i.Screener.annotation_aa0fp||"The change in price of a security from the closing price on one day to the closing price on the following trading day.",ST159:i.Screener.annotation_st159||"This is an estimation of the value of a business that is obtained by multiplying the number of shares outstanding by the current price of a share.",ST168:i.Screener.annotation_st168||"The highest price a security reached during the last 52 weeks.",ST169:i.Screener.annotation_st169||"The lowest price a security reached during the last 52 weeks.",HS05X:i.Screener.annotation_hs05x||"A fund's price/earnings ratio can act as a gauge of the fund's investment strategy in the current market climate, and whether it has a value or growth orientation. Companies in those industries enjoying a surge of popularity tend to have high P/E ratios, reflecting a growth orientation. More staid industries, tend to have low P/E ratios, reflecting a value orientation.  Morningstar generates this figure in-house on a monthly basis, based on the most-recent portfolio holdings submitted by the fund and stock statistics gleaned from our internal U.S. equities databases. Negative P/Es are not used, and any P/E greater than 60 is capped at 60 in the calculation of the average.",ST198:i.Screener.annotation_st198||"The most-recent stock price divided by the mean EPS estimate for the current fiscal year.",ST412:i.Screener.annotation_st412||"The most-recent stock price divided by the mean EPS estimate for the current fiscal year.",ST299:i.Screener.annotation_st299||"This percentage is calculated by dividing total dividends by the current price and multiplying by 100.  The total dividends are as of the most recent month-end, not the most recent fiscal quarter.",HS736:i.Screener.annotation_hs736||"A valuation ratio which compares a stock's per-share price to its book value (shareholders' equity). The price-to-book value ratio, expressed as a multiple (i.e. how many times a company's stock is trading per share compared to the company's book value per share), is an indication of how much shareholders are paying for the net assets of a company",HS737:i.Screener.annotation_hs737||"This represents the weighted average of the price/sales ratios of the stocks in a portfolio. Price/sales represents the amount an investor is willing to pay for a dollar generated from a particular company's operations. Price/sales (projected) is one of the five value factors used to calculate the Morningstar Style Box.",LF002:i.Screener.annotation_lf002||"Based on the ETF underlying holdings the investment is categorized into the following broad asset class categories; Commodities, International Stock, Money Market, Municipal Stock, Taxable Bond, U.S. Stock.",OF028:i.Screener.annotation_of028||"Categories help identify the top-performing funds, assess potential risk, and build well-diversified portfolios. Morningstar regularly reviews the category structure and the portfolios within each category to ensure that the system meets the needs of investors. In the United States, Morningstar supports 64 categories, which map into four broad asset class (U.S. Stock, International Stock, Taxable Bond, and Municipal Bond).",OF003:i.Screener.annotation_of003||"Categories help identify the top-performing funds, assess potential risk, and build well-diversified portfolios. Morningstar regularly reviews the category structure and the portfolios within each category to ensure that the system meets the needs of investors. In the United States, Morningstar supports 64 categories, which map into four broad asset class (U.S. Stock, International Stock, Taxable Bond, and Municipal Bond).",OS05P:i.Screener.annotation_os05p||"The percentage of fund assets, net of reimbursements,used to pay for operating expenses and management fees, including 12b-1 fees, administrative fees, and all other asset-based costs incurred by the fund, except brokerage costs. Fund expenses are reflected in the fund's NAV. Sales charges are not included in the expense ratio.",OF009:i.Screener.annotation_of009||"The month-end net assets of the mutual fund, recorded in millions of dollars.",OF00L:i.Screener.annotation_of00l||"The investment product's primary benchmark. For fund, the primary benchmark is stated in the fund's prospectus. If there is no primary benchmark explicitly stated, this field will be blank.",PM00A:i.Screener.annotation_pm00a||"Expressed in percentage terms, Morningstar's calculation of total return is determined each month by taking the change in monthly net asset value, reinvesting all income and capital-gains distributions during that month, and dividing by the starting NAV. Reinvestments are made using the actual reinvestment NAV, and daily payoffs are reinvested monthly. We provide both current and month-end returns",PM00C:i.Screener.annotation_pm00c||"Expressed in percentage terms, Morningstar's calculation of total return is determined each month by taking the change in monthly net asset value, reinvesting all income and capital-gains distributions during that month, and dividing by the starting NAV. Reinvestments are made using the actual reinvestment NAV, and daily payoffs are reinvested monthly. We provide both current and month-end returns",PM00E:i.Screener.annotation_pm00e||"Expressed in percentage terms, Morningstar's calculation of total return is determined each month by taking the change in monthly net asset value, reinvesting all income and capital-gains distributions during that month, and dividing by the starting NAV. Reinvestments are made using the actual reinvestment NAV, and daily payoffs are reinvested monthly. We provide both current and month-end returns",PD003:i.Screener.annotation_pd003||"Expressed in percentage terms, Morningstar's calculation of total return is determined each month by taking the change in monthly net asset value, reinvesting all income and capital-gains distributions during that month, and dividing by the starting NAV. Reinvestments are made using the actual reinvestment NAV, and daily payoffs are reinvested monthly. We provide both current and month-end returns",PM032:i.Screener.annotation_pm032||"Yield is a measure of the fund's income distributions, as a percentage of the fund price. Morningstar calculates this figure by summing the income distributions over the trailing 12 months and dividing that by the sum of the last month's ending NAV plus any capital gains distributed over the 12-month period. Morningstar adds back capital gains to estimate what the fund's ending price would have been had it not distributed these gains; this makes the result more comparable to a stock yield because stocks do not distribute gains but rather simply increase in price. ",HS008:i.Screener.annotation_hs008||"The net number of holdings in the portfolio.",HS07J:i.Screener.annotation_hs07j||"The aggregate assets, expressed as a percentage, of the fund's top 10 portfolio holdings. This figure is meant to be a measure of portfolio risk. Specifically, the higher the percentage, the more concentrated the fund is in a few companies or issues, and the more the fund is susceptible to the market fluctuations in these few holdings. Cash and cash equivalents are not included in this calculation. ",HS00L:i.Screener.annotation_hs00l||"Morningstar classifies bond funds in its style box based on their sensitivity to interest rate as limited, moderate and extensive measured by the average effective duration of the fund's holdings; and their average credit quality as high, medium, or low based on letter (or alphanumeric) credit ratings of bond holdings by third party credit rating agencies. The nine possible combinations of these characteristics correspond to the nine squares of the Morningstar Style Box – quality is displayed along the vertical axis and sensitivity to interest rate along the horizontal axis.",OS640:i.Screener.annotation_os640||"Denotes whether ETF is leveraged."}}}(QSAPI),function(t){var e=t.$,i=t.Util,a=t.QS_Lang,s=t.GB_SEARCH_DATASOURCE,n=t.GLOBAL_LOGIN_STATUS;i.isValid(t.GB_SECURITY_CODE,!0,!0,!0)||